depending only on samples before tB, and D_t the momentum noise (D_0 = G_0 - grad f(W_0), D_{t+1} = beta D_t + (1-beta)(G_{t+1} - grad f(W_{t+1})), G_t the batch mean on samples tB..tB+B-1; so D_t = M_t - C_t): D_t is measurable, depends only on samples before (t+1)B, ||D_t||^2 integrable, and E||D_t||^2 <= (sigma^2/B)(a + (1-a) beta^{2t}), a = (1-beta)/(1+beta). True: the cross term vanishes (noise_cross), so E||D_{t+1}||^2 <= beta^2 E||D_t||^2 + (1-beta)^2 sigma^2/B, and beta^2 a + (1-beta)^2 = a closes the induction. This is the exact (corrected) form of lines 585-588 (the source's beta^{t-i} typo is beta^{2(t-i)}).","id":"declaration:OracleTemplate.Muon.momentum_noise","source":["src_555842d1134266274751bccc","src_60fadde6ad80613d32896e45","src_dc6478c950ca730ea0987c12","src_d02eadaa16a3572f6afee7f9"],"verdict":"match"},{"candidate":["candidate/inventory/declarations/4/type_text"],"comparison":"Statement (registered sorry body, not evidence): For 0 <= beta < 1, 0 <= y <= 1, a = (1-beta)/(1+beta): sqrt(a + (1-a) y^2) <= sqrt(a) + beta y. True: squaring, it reduces to (1-a-beta^2) y <= 2 beta sqrt(a); 1-a-beta^2 = beta(1-beta)(2+beta)/(1+beta) and y <= 1, so it suffices that (1-beta)(2+beta)^2 <= 4(1+beta), i.e. -3beta^2 - beta^3 <= 4 beta. With y = beta^t it yields E||D_t|| <= (sigma/sqrt B)(sqrt a + beta^{t+1}), whose sum gives the source's beta/(1-beta) coefficient (accepted DMAIN route).","id":"declaration:OracleTemplate.Muon.momentum_sqrt_le","source":["src_d02eadaa16a3572f6afee7f9","src_7c316e9d345a24b0434c9235","src_b54153eb31a9e3b7c9623ea8"],"verdict":"match"},{"candidate":["candidate/inventory/declarations/5/type_text"],"comparison":"Statement (registered sorry body, not evidence): Common hypotheses: IID coordinates of P with marginal mu, IsStochasticGradient f g sigma under mu (Assumption 3.3 with joint measurability/integrability), gradient f measurable (true under the setups' differentiability; supplied by callers). For X, Y measurable and depending only on samples before k, with ||X||^2 integrable: <X, g(Y, s_k) - grad f(Y)> is integrable and has integral 0. True: integrability from |<a,b>| <= (||a||^2+||b||^2)/2 and noise_sq; zero mean by independence of s_k and unbiasedness int g(y, xi) dmu = grad f(y) for every y. This is the vanishing of cross terms used at lines 575 and 586.","id":"declaration:OracleTemplate.Muon.noise_cross","source":["src_293fe077ed407a88295c42d8","src_c2006baa6ea30939f6b9a763","src_555842d1134266274751bccc","src_60fadde6ad80613d32896e45"],"verdict":"match"},{"candidate":["candidate/inventory/declarations/6/type_text"],"comparison":"Statement (registered sorry body, not evidence): Common hypotheses: IID coordinates of P with marginal mu, IsStochasticGradient f g sigma under mu (Assumption 3.3 with joint measurability/integrability), gradient f measurable (true under the setups' differentiability; supplied by callers). For Y measurable and depending only on samples s_j, j < k: s |-> ||g(Y s, s_k) - grad f(Y s)||^2 is integrable with integral <= sigma^2. True: s_k is independent of (s_j)_{j<k} with law mu, so the integral equals E[phi(Y)] with phi(y) = int ||g(y,xi) - grad f(y)||^2 dmu <= sigma^2 (variance assumption for every y).","id":"declaration:OracleTemplate.Muon.noise_sq","source":["src_293fe077ed407a88295c42d8","src_c2006baa6ea30939f6b9a763","src_bc5f0f02387e648e447be892","src_4b2b38c364168ed4ffbf2578"],"verdict":"match"},{"candidate":["candidate/inventory/declarations/5/type_text","candidate/inventory/declarations/1/type_text","candidate/inventory/declarations/3/type_text","candidate/inventory/declarations/2/type_text","candidate/inventory/declarations/4/type_text","candidate/unit/node_declarations/DSTOCH"],"comparison":"DSTOCH maps to five theorems with true statements matching the accepted contract; noise_sq and batch_mean_sub are true helpers. No definitions owned.","id":"node:DSTOCH","source":["src_92920d1a64334a467758b735","src_4886eb4e59cbb294d31e2a6d","src_a892cc5b3718d4989f